#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb69acaf-e7b1-5296-b5cd-e351f7765090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ + \\cos θ\\sin 2θ - 2\\sin θ\\cos 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122829} {:answer/id #uuid "cf907968-aa54-5a22-bf1f-ba61126aa8a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ + \\sin θ\\sin 2θ + 2\\cos θ\\cos 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122830} {:answer/id #uuid "f7646237-8e2b-5da3-97dc-e3f56b8cc436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ\\sin 2θ + 2\\cos θ\\cos 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122831} {:answer/id #uuid "16c0e9ea-c44f-5823-94da-7eddeb930809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ\\sin 2θ + 2\\cos θ\\cos 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122832} {:answer/id #uuid "351bf10a-bb59-5a2e-b931-d20c9da03892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ + \\cos θ\\sin 2θ - 2\\sin θ\\cos 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122833}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f8e063-53b4-59b1-a70a-2cbd7cdb3bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122828}] :question/id #uuid "c985905c-7d24-536e-9cc0-8e7d7e1b4d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-37186" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, θ) = r(1 + \\sin 2θ)$, find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d87c61-ce4c-51b4-a48d-e8a1bd1e34a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842} {:answer/id #uuid "e8df0f85-5951-5810-997c-6d9f9ec513ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122843} {:answer/id #uuid "29657135-0af4-5058-ad50-f0440efad395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r\\cos θ + r\\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122844} {:answer/id #uuid "2bad1c34-a8db-52f0-b044-93c8515a9174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122845}] :answer-field/id #uuid "086dfdbb-92b2-5cae-9238-3f1a3c6feab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840}] :question/id #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-37196" :question/problem "Given that $g(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e906c41-889d-5cd4-ad89-16c03fc6164e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos θ + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124083} {:answer/id #uuid "ba459fbc-4548-5ad4-89aa-d1182aa1f99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124084} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a6e4db-ca55-519b-a04f-9f443bb2111e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860